---
import LetsTalk from '@components/home/LetsTalk.astro'
import Insight from '@components/insights/Insight.astro'
import InsightSignoff from '@components/insights/InsightSignoff.astro'
import Layout from '@layouts/Layout.astro'
import { getInsightNav } from '@data/insights'
import InsightNav from '@components/insights/InsightNav.astro'

const { previous, current, next } = getInsightNav('do-i-need-a-mobile-app-or-is-a-mobile-friendly-website-enough')
---

<Layout title={current?.title} description={current?.summary}>
  <Insight tags={current?.tags}>
    <span slot="insight-title">{current?.title}</span>
    <span slot="insight-subtitle">{current?.dateLabel}</span>
    <span slot="insight-text">
      <p>
        Every business owner asks this eventually: build a proper app, or keep everything on a mobile-friendly
        website? The answer is usually the website, but there are a handful of situations where an app is genuinely
        the right call.
      </p><br />
      <p>
        How often will someone actually use it? If this is something people touch once or twice a year, like
        checking your hours or filling out a contact form, a website wins. If it is something people would open
        daily, an app starts to make sense.
      </p><br />
      <p>
        Does it need to work without a reliable connection? Apps can cache data and function offline. A website
        generally cannot, unless it is built as a progressive web app. This matters most for people working in the
        field: tradespeople, warehouse staff, delivery drivers.
      </p><br />
      <p>
        Does it need access to hardware a browser cannot reach? Camera, GPS, Bluetooth, biometrics, background
        location. If the product genuinely depends on this, a website cannot do it properly. Push notifications
        fall into this category too: web push exists, but adoption and reliability, especially on iOS, still lag
        well behind native push.
      </p><br />
      <p>
        How will people actually find it? Websites get found through search. Apps get found through app stores, or
        because someone was told to go and download it. A website delivers value the moment someone clicks a link.
        An app requires someone to decide to install something before they get anything at all, which is a much
        higher barrier.
      </p><br />
      <p>
        Would someone actually want this on their home screen? People are genuinely reluctant to install an app for
        something minor. If you cannot clearly answer why someone would want your icon sitting on their phone, that
        is usually a sign you do not need one.
      </p><br />
      <p>
        There is also a middle ground most businesses do not know about: a progressive web app can offer an
        install-to-homescreen icon and some offline capability, without the cost or complexity of building and
        maintaining a full native app. If you are weighing this up for your own business, that is exactly the kind
        of decision worth talking through before you commit to a build, through a short consulting conversation.
      </p><br />
      <InsightSignoff />
    </span>
  </Insight>

  <InsightNav previousInsight={previous} nextInsight={next} />

  <LetsTalk config={{ buttons: 'both', primaryCta: 'message' }} />
</Layout>
